# MS-DOS batch files for the Victor 9000 harness MUST arrive with CRLF line
# endings.  Written with Unix \n, COMMAND.COM on Victor MS-DOS 3.1 echoes
# every line and executes none of them, with a staircase display that looks
# like a corrupted terminal rather than a corrupted file.  It is the first
# item on PORTING.md SS16a's landmine list, and SS16ae stepped on it anyway
# and lost a round of bench legs to it.
#
# "text eol=crlf" is deliberate and is stronger than marking them binary.
# Binary would preserve whatever bytes happen to be committed; this
# normalises to LF in the repository and converts to CRLF on EVERY
# checkout, so a .BAT authored on any platform, by any editor, with any
# core.autocrlf setting, lands on the image in the form the Victor needs.
# The guarantee is what is wanted here -- these files are generated by
# tooling as often as they are edited by hand.
#
# No .bat/.BAT file elsewhere in this tree is tracked (upstream C-Kermit
# has none), so this rule cannot disturb anything but the harness.
*.BAT text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
